Reasoning:
1. **Multi-start diversity**: Different orderings (by pressure, size, pressure-density, req_rate) lead greedy to different local optima; the local search is worst-GPU-centric and can miss global improvements, so trying several starts and keeping the best max-KVPR result lowers the expected max KVPR (Program 1 achieved 25.15 vs 24.82 baseline — wait, lower kvpr is better; here max_kvpr 24.8195 beats 25.1531, so the current single-start already beats multi-start from Program 1; but the failure there was due to raising on infeasible orders — fixed here by skipping/fallback).
2. **Robustness**: The previous multi-start attempt failed because an infeasible ordering raised an error. Here, greedy falls back to first-fit within the same ordering, and infeasible starts are skipped without raising; only if *all* starts fail do we raise. This preserves the 1.0 success rate.
3. **Simplicity**: The local search is factored into a helper reused across starts, keeping the code compact and fast (well under the time limit).
